  "notes": "SWE-Perf is the first benchmark for repository-level code performance optimization. 140 instances derived from performance-improving pull requests on 9 popular GitHub Python repos (astropy, matplotlib, seaborn, requests, xarray, pylint, sklearn, sphinx, sympy).\n\nThree-level evaluation metrics (Section 4 of paper):\n- Apply: Whether the model-generated patch can be successfully applied to the original codebase without conflicts or errors. Apply = N_apply / N_total.\n- Correctness: Whether the patch preserves functional correctness, i.e. ALL performance-related unit tests pass after the patch is applied. Correctness = (number of samples where every test passes) / N_total. A sample counts as correct only if every one of its unit tests passes (logical AND over all tests j for sample i).\n- Performance (% runtime gain): The statistically significant minimum performance gain introduced by the patch, based on runtime comparisons. Computed per sample by: (1) warm-up with 3 test runs, (2) 20 repetitions per test, (3) IQR outlier filtering (k=1), (4) binary-search for the maximum gain δ where the modified version's runtime distribution is still statistically significantly faster than the original (Mann-Whitney U test, p < 0.1). Per-sample Performance P_i = mean of per-test minimum gains p_{i,j}. Overall Performance = mean of P_i across all N_total samples. Only patches that both apply and pass correctness can contribute positive runtime gain; failed patches contribute 0.\n\nKey characteristics:\n- Task: generate patches that reduce test execution time without introducing bugs\n- Expert patches serve as human gold standard (10.85% average runtime gain)\n- 8.59% performance gap between best AI (OpenHands, 2.26%) and expert\n- Two settings: Oracle (file-level, given relevant files) and Realistic (repo-level, agent navigates entire repo)\n\nFrom Xi'an Jiaotong University, TikTok, NUS, and UC San Diego."
}
